<h2 id=intro>Introduction</h2>

*This section is non-normative.*

Operating systems let users adjust how apps look in ways that web pages can't follow. On iOS and iPadOS, users
can ask for bold text, for buttons to be drawn with shapes instead of plain colored text, for information to be
shown without relying on color, and for switches to show on and off labels. On Windows, macOS, and Linux
desktops, users can stop the text cursor from blinking. Native apps follow these settings. A web app that draws
its own switches, links, charts, and editor caret can't.

[[MEDIAQUERIES-5]] already has features for some accessibility settings, such as
'@media/prefers-reduced-motion' and '@media/prefers-contrast'. This specification adds five more:

<div class=example id=example-intro>
    <pre highlight=css>
    @media (prefers-button-shapes: show) {
      a, .link-button { text-decoration-line: underline; }
      .icon-button { outline: 1px solid currentColor; outline-offset: 2px; }
    }

    @media (prefers-differentiate-without-color: differentiate) {
      .field-error { text-decoration: wavy underline; }
      .chart .series-2 { stroke-dasharray: 6 3; }
    }

    @media (prefers-on-off-labels: show) {
      .switch .state-glyph { color: inherit; } /* transparent otherwise */
    }

    @media (prefers-reduced-caret-blink: reduce) {
      .code-editor { caret-animation: manual; }
    }

    @media (prefers-bold-text: bold) {
      .display-heading { -webkit-text-stroke: 0.03em currentColor; }
    }
    </pre>
</div>

These settings are used by many people, and they are also used by people with low vision, color vision
deficiency, dyslexia, epilepsy, and cognitive disabilities. A page that could read them could guess at those
conditions. So each feature is a protected media feature [[!MEDIA-FEATURE-PRIVACY]]: by default, rules that depend
on it can change only [=paint-only properties=], like the ones above, and nothing the page can measure changes.
The user can choose to share a setting with sites, and then pages can change their layout as well.


<h2 id=features>Media features</h2>

Each feature in this section is a [=protected media feature=] whose [=observable value=] is
''no-preference''. In each, ''no-preference'' evaluates to false in a [=boolean context=], and the other value
evaluates to true.

The user agent must derive each feature's [=actual value=] only from the setting it describes, whether that
setting is in the operating system or in the user agent. It must not derive any of them from whether assistive
technology is running, or from any other setting.

<div class=note>
    User agents should also follow these settings in what they draw themselves, such as native form controls
    and the text caret, as native apps do. The features are for what pages draw.
</div>

<h3 id=bold-text>The '@media/prefers-bold-text' feature</h3>

<pre class='descdef mq'>
Name: prefers-bold-text
Value: no-preference | bold
For: @media
Type: discrete
</pre>

: <dfn value for=@media/prefers-bold-text>bold</dfn>
:: The user has asked for text to be drawn with heavier weights, such as with the Bold Text setting on iOS,
    iPadOS, and Android.
: <dfn value for=@media/prefers-bold-text>no-preference</dfn>
:: Otherwise.

<h3 id=button-shapes>The '@media/prefers-button-shapes' feature</h3>

<pre class='descdef mq'>
Name: prefers-button-shapes
Value: no-preference | show
For: @media
Type: discrete
</pre>

: <dfn value for=@media/prefers-button-shapes>show</dfn>
:: The user has asked for links and buttons to be identified by a visible shape, such as an underline, outline,
    or background, rather than by color or position alone. Examples are the Button Shapes setting on iOS and
    iPadOS, the setting that shows toolbar button shapes on macOS, and user agent settings that always underline
    links.
: <dfn value for=@media/prefers-button-shapes>no-preference</dfn>
:: Otherwise.

<h3 id=differentiate-without-color>The '@media/prefers-differentiate-without-color' feature</h3>

<pre class='descdef mq'>
Name: prefers-differentiate-without-color
Value: no-preference | differentiate
For: @media
Type: discrete
</pre>

: <dfn value for=@media/prefers-differentiate-without-color>differentiate</dfn>
:: The user has asked for information shown with color to be shown in another way as well, such as with a
    shape, pattern, or text, as with the Differentiate Without Color setting on iOS, iPadOS, and macOS.
: <dfn value for=@media/prefers-differentiate-without-color>no-preference</dfn>
:: Otherwise.

<h3 id=on-off-labels>The '@media/prefers-on-off-labels' feature</h3>

<pre class='descdef mq'>
Name: prefers-on-off-labels
Value: no-preference | show
For: @media
Type: discrete
</pre>

: <dfn value for=@media/prefers-on-off-labels>show</dfn>
:: The user has asked for switches to show a label or symbol for their state, as with the On/Off Labels setting
    on iOS and iPadOS.
: <dfn value for=@media/prefers-on-off-labels>no-preference</dfn>
:: Otherwise.

<h3 id=reduced-caret-blink>The '@media/prefers-reduced-caret-blink' feature</h3>

<pre class='descdef mq'>
Name: prefers-reduced-caret-blink
Value: no-preference | reduce
For: @media
Type: discrete
</pre>

: <dfn value for=@media/prefers-reduced-caret-blink>reduce</dfn>
:: The user has turned off blinking of the text caret, such as with the cursor blink rate setting on Windows,
    the setting for a non-blinking text insertion pointer on macOS, or the cursor blinking setting in GNOME.
: <dfn value for=@media/prefers-reduced-caret-blink>no-preference</dfn>
:: Otherwise, including when the caret blinks at any rate.


<h2 id=evaluation>Evaluation</h2>

<h3 id=sharing>Sharing a setting</h3>

For each of the features, the user agent keeps a <dfn>sharing choice</dfn>, which is <dfn>shared</dfn> or
<dfn>private</dfn>. The user agent must let the user change it. It may offer one choice for all five features, or
one for each.

<div class=note>
    For example, a user agent's accessibility settings could say: "Websites adapt to your accessibility
    settings. Let websites detect them, so they can also change their layout?"
</div>

A user agent should set the sharing choice for a feature to [=private=] by default. It may set it to [=shared=] by
default for a feature that it has good reason to believe is turned on by at least one in sixteen of its users on
that kind of device.

<div class=note>
    At one in sixteen, a user with the setting on reveals 4 bits by sharing it. Settings that fewer people use
    reveal more, and are more likely to reveal a disability. See [[#privacy-fingerprinting]].
</div>

<h3 id=which-evaluation>Full and protected evaluation</h3>

A [=document=] uses [=full evaluation=] for a feature when all of these are true:

* The feature's [=sharing choice=] is [=shared=].
* The document is [=allowed to use=] "<dfn export>accessibility-preferences</dfn>", a policy-controlled
    feature whose default allowlist is <code>&#39;self&#39;</code>.
* The document isn't in a [=reduced-exposure context=].
* Scripting is enabled for the document.

Otherwise, the document uses [=protected evaluation=] for the feature.

<div class=note>
    This is [=frame-scoped exposure=], with protected evaluation instead of a fixed value for frames that aren't
    allowed. Cross-origin frames, documents without script, and private browsing modes still adapt their
    appearance to the user's settings. They just can't find out what the settings are.
</div>

<h3 id=changes>Changes</h3>

In documents that use full evaluation, the features use [=delayed change delivery=], with a [=maximum delay=] of
60 seconds and a [=minimum interval=] of 10 minutes. In documents that use protected evaluation, a change is
painted immediately, because it isn't observable.

<h3 id=user-control>Turning features off</h3>

The features are [=under user control=]. The user agent must let the user turn a feature off for the web
without changing the setting it describes. When a feature is off, its [=actual value=] is ''no-preference'' in
every document.


<h2 id=authoring>Authoring guidance</h2>

*This section is non-normative.*

Most users will have the [=private=] sharing choice, so authors should first adapt with the
[=paint-only properties=], which work everywhere, and use layout changes only as an addition for users who share
their settings.

<table class=data>
    <thead><tr><th>Feature<th>Works with paint-only properties<th>Needs the setting to be shared
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>'@media/prefers-bold-text'
            <td>A heavier stroke with '-webkit-text-stroke-width'; stronger 'color' contrast
            <td>A heavier 'font-weight', which changes line lengths
        <tr><td>'@media/prefers-button-shapes'
            <td>Underlines, 'outline', 'box-shadow', 'background-color'
            <td>Borders and padding
        <tr><td>'@media/prefers-differentiate-without-color'
            <td>Underline styles, 'outline-style', 'stroke-dasharray', 'fill' patterns defined in the document,
                repeating gradients
            <td>Added icons or text
        <tr><td>'@media/prefers-on-off-labels'
            <td>Showing a label that is always present, by changing its 'color' from ''transparent''
            <td>Inserting a label with 'content' or 'display'
        <tr><td>'@media/prefers-reduced-caret-blink'
            <td>'caret-animation', 'caret-color', 'caret-shape'
            <td>Stopping a caret drawn by the page with 'animation'
</table>

<div class=example id=example-switch>
    A switch whose state glyphs are always in the markup, marked <code>aria-hidden</code> because the switch's
    state is already exposed through its role, and are revealed only by color:

    <pre highlight=html>
    &lt;button role="switch" aria-checked="true" class="switch">
      &lt;span class="state-glyph" aria-hidden="true">I&lt;/span>
    &lt;/button>
    </pre>

    <pre highlight=css>
    .switch .state-glyph { color: transparent; }
    @media (prefers-on-off-labels: show) {
      .switch .state-glyph { color: inherit; }
    }
    </pre>
</div>


<h2 id=privacy>Privacy considerations</h2>

<h3 id=privacy-sensitive>What the settings reveal</h3>

Each setting is used by people without disabilities, and each is used disproportionately by people with them:

<table class=data>
    <thead><tr><th>Setting<th>Commonly used by people with
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>Bold text<td>Low vision, age-related difficulty focusing on near objects, dyslexia
        <tr><td>Button shapes<td>Low vision, cognitive and learning disabilities
        <tr><td>Differentiate without color<td>Color vision deficiency, which is inherited, and is far more
            common in men than in women
        <tr><td>On/off labels<td>Color vision deficiency, cognitive disabilities
        <tr><td>Reduced caret blink<td>Photosensitive epilepsy, migraine, attention disorders, vestibular
            disorders
</table>

A site that could read these settings could infer health information, which data protection law often treats as
a special category [[GDPR]]; could infer age and sex; and could use either to exclude, target, or charge users
differently.

<h3 id=privacy-fingerprinting>Fingerprinting</h3>

Each setting is uncommon, and uncommon values identify users. A setting that 2% of users turn on reveals 5.6 bits
about each of them, and one that 0.5% turn on reveals 7.6 bits. Settings used by the same people tend to be turned
on together, which reduces the sum, but a user with three of the five settings on may still reveal well over 10
bits. The users who reveal the most are the users with disabilities.

<h3 id=privacy-design>How the features are protected</h3>

<table class=data>
    <thead><tr><th>Channel<th>Private (default)<th>Shared
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>Script: <code>matchMedia()</code>, computed style
            <td>Always ''no-preference''
            <td>The setting
        <tr><td>Layout
            <td>No change
            <td>Changes
        <tr><td>Network
            <td>No difference in what is fetched
            <td>Resources can be fetched only under one value
        <tr><td>Timing of changes
            <td>Not observable
            <td>Delayed change delivery
        <tr><td>Cross-origin frames
            <td>Protected evaluation
            <td>Protected evaluation, unless the embedding page allows "accessibility-preferences"
        <tr><td>Documents without script; private browsing
            <td>Protected evaluation
            <td>Protected evaluation
</table>

With the [=private=] sharing choice, the only ways for a page to learn the settings are the ones described in
[[#privacy-residual]]. Even with the [=shared=] choice, cross-origin frames, documents without script, and private
browsing modes can't learn them.

<h3 id=privacy-why-not-always-private>Why sharing is possible at all</h3>

Some adaptations need layout: text drawn at a heavier weight takes more room and has to wrap differently, and a
label that isn't in the page already has to be inserted. Protected evaluation can't allow those without revealing
the setting. A user who wants the full adaptation, and accepts that sites can learn the setting, can choose to
share it.

<h3 id=privacy-why-not-always-shared>Why sharing isn't the default</h3>

Existing accessibility media features, such as '@media/prefers-reduced-motion', are revealed to every page, and some
browsers turn them off in private browsing modes to protect users from fingerprinting, which also takes away the
adaptation. Protected evaluation keeps the adaptation and removes the fingerprint. For settings that few people
use, and that reveal disability, the default should be the one that doesn't make the users who need them easier
to identify.

<h3 id=privacy-at>Assistive technology</h3>

These are settings, not assistive technology, and [[#features]] forbids deriving them from whether assistive
technology is running. A user of a screen reader who also has Button Shapes turned on reveals, at most, that
setting.

<h3 id=privacy-third-party>Embedded content</h3>

Cross-origin frames use protected evaluation unless the page that embeds them allows "accessibility-preferences".
Embedded content therefore still adapts its colors, outlines, and caret to the user, and a tracker embedded in many
sites can't read the settings. An embedding page should allow the feature only for frames that need layout
adaptations, such as an embedded editor.

<h3 id=privacy-without-script>Reading the features without script</h3>

A style sheet in an email or a page with script blocked could report the settings to a server by loading a
resource under the query. Documents without script always use protected evaluation, in which no resource is
fetched differently, so this isn't possible.

<h3 id=privacy-correlation>Linking visits by when a setting changes</h3>

Users rarely change these settings. When they do, documents that use protected evaluation can't observe it, and
documents that use full evaluation learn of it through [[#changes|delayed change delivery]].

<h3 id=privacy-residual>Remaining risks</h3>

* **Interaction.** A page could paint something only for users with a setting, and learn the setting if the user
    reacts. This requires tricking the user, and works one user at a time.
* **Users who share.** A user who shares a setting reveals it to top-level documents and to frames their
    embedders allow, including first-party scripts from trackers.
* **Captures the user shares.** A page that the user lets capture its own tab can see what was painted.
* **Native controls.** User agents that follow a setting in native controls should make sure a page can't measure
    the difference, for example if an on/off label changes a native switch's size.


<h2 id=security>Security considerations</h2>

* **Targeting.** A page could show different content to users with a setting that suggests age or disability,
    such as a scam shown only to users with bold text. With protected evaluation, such content can only differ in
    color, outlines, and decoration, and the server can't select it. With a shared setting, it could differ more.
    Services that check pages for fraud should check them with these settings on.
* **Hiding content.** A page could use a paint-only property to hide content from users with a setting. A page
    can already hide anything it wants.
* **No new capabilities.** None of the features fetch anything, run script, or change what the user can do.


<h2 id=alternate-proposals>Alternate proposals</h2>

<h3 id=alt-bundle>One feature for all explicit cues</h3>

A single feature, such as <code>(prefers-explicit-cues)</code>, could cover button shapes, differentiate without
color, and on/off labels. It would reveal one bit instead of up to three. It was not chosen because the settings
are independent, and a single feature would make authors apply all three adaptations for any one of them. With
protected evaluation as the default, the number of bits matters less.

<h3 id=alt-always-private>Never allowing full evaluation</h3>

The features could always use protected evaluation. That would protect every user, and would make adaptations
that need layout impossible. See [[#privacy-why-not-always-private]] and the open issues.

<h3 id=alt-client-hints>Client hints</h3>

Client hints such as those proposed for other user preference features would let servers send adapted pages. They
were not chosen because they reveal the settings to servers, which is what protected evaluation prevents.

<h3 id=alt-not-included>Settings not included</h3>

* **Captions.** Operating systems have a setting to show captions. Turning captions on for a video is done in
    script, not CSS, so a media feature wouldn't help.
* **Whether a screen reader is running.** This would reveal the use of assistive technology directly, and is
    deliberately not proposed.
* **Text size.** Other proposals, such as an environment variable for the user's preferred text scale, cover it.


<h2 id=open-issues>Open issues</h2>

<p class=issue>Should full evaluation be removed, so the features are always protected? Accessibility reviewers
and privacy reviewers may weigh the layout adaptations differently.

<p class=issue>Is one in sixteen the right threshold for a default of [=shared=], and how should a user agent
estimate how many of its users have a setting on without collecting that data?

<p class=issue>Should the protections in this specification also apply to '@media/prefers-reduced-motion',
'@media/prefers-contrast', and '@media/prefers-reduced-transparency'? Many of the adaptations for them, such as
stopping animations, aren't paint-only.


<h2 id=questionnaire class=no-num>Appendix: Security and privacy questionnaire</h2>

*This section is non-normative.* These are the answers to the questions in
[[SECURITY-PRIVACY-QUESTIONNAIRE]].

: What information does this feature expose, and for what purposes?
:: With the default private choice, none: pages adapt their appearance without observing the settings. With the
    shared choice, five accessibility settings, so pages can also adapt their layout.
: Do features in your specification expose the minimum amount of information necessary?
:: Yes. Adaptations that don't need the value don't get it.
: Do the features expose personal information, PII, or information derived from either?
:: When shared, the settings can suggest health conditions, age, and sex. See [[#privacy-sensitive]].
: How do the features deal with sensitive information?
:: Protected evaluation by default, and always in frames, documents without script, and private modes.
: Does data exposed carry related but distinct information that may not be obvious to users?
:: Yes, disability and age. The sharing choice should say so.
: Do the features introduce state that persists across browsing sessions?
:: No. The sharing choice is a user agent setting, not visible to pages.
: Do the features expose information about the underlying platform to origins?
:: The state of operating system accessibility settings, when shared.
: Does this specification allow an origin to send data to the underlying platform?
:: No.
: Do features enable access to device sensors?
:: No.
: Do features enable new script execution or loading mechanisms?
:: No.
: Do features allow an origin to access other devices?
:: No.
: Do features allow an origin some measure of control over a user agent's native UI?
:: No.
: What temporary identifiers do the features create or expose to the web?
:: None. Changes are either unobservable or delayed.
: How does this specification distinguish between behavior in first-party and third-party contexts?
:: Cross-origin frames use protected evaluation unless allowed "accessibility-preferences".
: How do the features work in the context of a browser's Private Browsing or Incognito mode?
:: Modes that protect against fingerprinting are reduced-exposure contexts, which use protected evaluation. Pages
    still adapt their appearance.
: Does this specification have both "Security Considerations" and "Privacy Considerations" sections?
:: Yes.
: Do features enable origins to downgrade default security protections?
:: No.
: What happens when a document that uses your feature is kept alive in BFCache?
:: In full evaluation, it gets any change when it becomes visible. In protected evaluation, it is painted with the
    current settings.
: What happens when a document that uses your feature gets disconnected?
:: Nothing is kept.
: Does your spec define when and how new kinds of errors should be raised?
:: No.
: Does your feature allow sites to learn about the user's use of assistive technology?
:: No. The settings must not be derived from assistive technology, and aren't observable unless the user shares
    them.
: What should this questionnaire have asked?
:: Whether a feature that helps people with disabilities makes those people easier to identify than other users,
    and whether it still works for them when privacy protections are on.
